WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.638
In part 3, we counted the words in a line of text.

00:00:03.638 --> 00:00:09.380
But a dictionary of counts doesn't tell you, at a glance, which words are most common.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.410
So how do you sort a dictionary by its values?

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.560
The answer uses our third collection, called a tuple.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:23.213
This is part 4 of Lecture 5: tuples, and sorting a dictionary.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.029
A tuple is another kind of sequence, written with round brackets.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:29.640
It works much like a list.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.207
The elements are indexed from 0, so X of 2 is Joseph.

00:00:34.207 --> 00:00:37.470
Max works, and you can loop over a tuple.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.236
But tuples are immutable, like strings.

00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:44.213
A list lets you change an element.

00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:45.702
A string doesn't.

00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:51.220
And neither does a tuple: assigning to Z of 2 is a `TypeError`.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.729
So none of the list methods that change a list exist for a tuple.

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:59.524
No `sort`, no `append`, no `reverse`.

00:00:59.524 --> 00:01:03.300
A tuple has only two methods: `count` and `index`.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.511
That makes tuples simpler, and a little more efficient.

00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:12.987
More importantly, a tuple tells the reader: this group of values won't change.

00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:17.690
We use tuples for coordinates, small records, and key, value pairs.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.168
One detail: it's the comma that makes a tuple.

00:01:22.168 --> 00:01:25.309
5 in brackets is just the number 5.

00:01:25.309 --> 00:01:28.360
5, comma, in brackets, is a tuple.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:32.256
A tuple can stand on the left of an assignment.

00:01:32.256 --> 00:01:37.173
Python assigns each element on the right to the name in the same position.

00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:45.403
So X, Y equals 4, fred makes X 4, and Y fred.

00:01:45.753 --> 00:01:47.789
This gives a neat swap.

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:49.294
A, B equals B, A.

00:01:49.294 --> 00:01:55.313
The right side is built first, and then unpacked into the two names.

00:01:55.663 --> 00:02:01.424
And it's exactly what happens in `for key, value in counts.items()`.

00:02:01.424 --> 00:02:09.303
Each item is a key, value tuple, and tuple assignment unpacks it into the two loop variables.

00:02:09.653 --> 00:02:12.252
`items` gives a view of these tuples.

00:02:12.252 --> 00:02:15.973
Wrap it in `list`, and you get a real list of tuples.

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:19.200
Being immutable has one more use.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.887
A tuple can be a dictionary key, but a list can't.

00:02:23.237 --> 00:02:27.412
A dictionary finds a key by its value at the moment it was stored.

00:02:27.412 --> 00:02:32.157
If the key could change afterwards, the dictionary could no longer find it.

00:02:32.507 --> 00:02:38.034
So keys must be immutable: numbers, strings, and tuples of those.

00:02:38.034 --> 00:02:44.667
Try a list as a key, and Python raises a `TypeError`: unhashable type, `list`.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:47.026
Tuples can be compared.

00:02:47.026 --> 00:02:49.449
Python compares the first elements.

00:02:49.449 --> 00:02:55.057
If they're equal, it goes on to the second, and so on, until two elements differ.

00:02:55.057 --> 00:02:56.234
Those two decide.

00:02:56.584 --> 00:03:02.464
(0, 1, 2) is less than (5, 1, 2), because 0 is less than 5.

00:03:02.814 --> 00:03:08.761
Here, the first elements are equal, so the second ones decide: 1 is less than 3.

00:03:08.761 --> 00:03:11.214
The 2 million is never looked at.

00:03:11.564 --> 00:03:13.500
Strings work the same way.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:20.204
('Jones', 'Sally') is not less than ('Jones', 'Fred'), because 'Sally' comes after 'Fred'.

00:03:21.001 --> 00:03:22.942
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:22.942 --> 00:03:26.339
Is ('Jones', 'Sally') less than ('Jones', 'Sam')?

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.281
And which characters decide?

00:03:28.831 --> 00:03:29.211
True.

00:03:29.211 --> 00:03:36.210
The first elements are equal, so Python compares 'Sally' with 'Sam', character by character.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.471
The S's match, the A's match, and then L comes before M.

00:03:41.268 --> 00:03:45.872
Because tuples can be compared, a list of tuples can be sorted.

00:03:45.872 --> 00:03:49.308
That gives us a sorted version of a dictionary.

00:03:49.658 --> 00:03:51.722
Get the items as a list.

00:03:51.722 --> 00:03:56.538
They come out in the order the keys were added: C, A, B.

00:03:56.888 --> 00:04:02.888
Sort the list, and the tuples are ordered by their first element, the key: A, B, C.

00:04:03.238 --> 00:04:06.464
The built-in function `sorted` does the same job,

00:04:06.464 --> 00:04:10.678
but it returns a new sorted list, and leaves the original alone.

00:04:11.028 --> 00:04:14.535
Both `sort` and `sorted` take `reverse=True`,

00:04:14.535 --> 00:04:17.108
to sort from largest to smallest.

00:04:17.458 --> 00:04:21.834
So don't count on a dictionary's order being the order you want.

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:24.228
It's the order the keys were added.

00:04:24.228 --> 00:04:25.938
When order matters, sort.

00:04:26.735 --> 00:04:31.415
Now, the practice from the slides: sort a dictionary by its values.

00:04:31.765 --> 00:04:35.286
Tuples are sorted by their first element.

00:04:35.286 --> 00:04:39.925
So build tuples with the value first: value, then key.

00:04:40.525 --> 00:04:45.525
Go through the items in a loop, and append each pair the other way round.

00:04:45.875 --> 00:04:48.100
Sort with `reverse=True`,

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.995
and the largest value comes first: 22, then 10, then 1.

00:04:53.345 --> 00:04:56.845
This pattern is called decorate, sort, undecorate.

00:04:56.845 --> 00:05:02.305
Put what you want to sort by first, sort, and then take out the part you need.

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:05.622
Now let's put the whole lecture together,

00:05:05.622 --> 00:05:08.142
and find the most common words in a text.

00:05:08.492 --> 00:05:12.092
First, count the words with a dictionary and `get`.

00:05:12.442 --> 00:05:15.922
Then turn each item into a (count, word) tuple.

00:05:16.272 --> 00:05:21.232
Sort them, largest first, and return the first N with a slice.

00:05:21.582 --> 00:05:26.560
On these 3 lines, the most common word is 'the', 7 times.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.142
Next come 'car', and 'and', 3 times each.

00:05:30.492 --> 00:05:34.367
('car', 'and') tie on the count, so the words decide.

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:39.852
With `reverse=True`, the word that comes later in the alphabet comes first.

00:05:40.202 --> 00:05:44.813
Notice that `top_words` returns its result, and the caller prints it.

00:05:44.813 --> 00:05:50.562
So you can test it on a 3-line list, like this one, before you point it at a big file.

00:05:50.912 --> 00:05:54.039
And because a file is also a sequence of lines,

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:57.432
the same function counts the words in a whole file.

00:05:58.202 --> 00:06:02.202
Before we finish, let's put the 3 collections side by side.

00:06:02.552 --> 00:06:05.535
A list is ordered, and you can change it.

00:06:05.535 --> 00:06:07.792
You look values up by position.

00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:10.759
A dictionary maps keys to values.

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:15.202
You can change it, it keeps its keys in insertion order,

00:06:15.202 --> 00:06:17.582
and you look values up by key.

00:06:17.932 --> 00:06:22.093
A tuple is ordered, like a list, but you can't change it.

00:06:22.093 --> 00:06:24.867
That's why it can be a dictionary key,

00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:28.372
and why it suits a small, fixed group of values.

00:06:28.722 --> 00:06:31.473
Whichever you use, test the empty case:

00:06:31.473 --> 00:06:35.002
an empty list, an empty dictionary, an empty line.

00:06:35.769 --> 00:06:36.969
Let's sum up.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:41.317
A tuple is an ordered sequence you can't change.

00:06:41.317 --> 00:06:46.399
Tuple assignment unpacks it, as in `for key, value in items`.

00:06:46.749 --> 00:06:49.200
Tuples compare element by element,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.589
so a list of tuples sorts by its first element.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:57.659
To sort a dictionary by value, put the value first, then sort.

00:06:58.009 --> 00:06:59.517
Here's one to try.

00:06:59.517 --> 00:07:04.966
Change `top_words` so that ties are broken in alphabetical order.

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:11.169
Hint: sort tuples of (minus the count, and the word), in the normal order.

00:07:11.719 --> 00:07:13.845
That's the end of Lecture 5.

00:07:13.845 --> 00:07:17.719
Next time: recursion, a function that calls itself.

